    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1017/s0022143000009254",
    "abstract": "Abstract A numerical model has been developed to simulate energy and mass evolution of snow cover at a given location, as a function of meteorological conditions: precipitation, air temperature, humidity, wind velocity, and incoming short-wave and long-wave radiation. This model, named CROCUS, was first tested on a well-instrumented field during a whole winter, showing its ability to simulate the important phenomena affecting the evolution of the snow layers: high temperature gradients, wetting, compaction, and melting-freezing cycles. A second test was conducted at two locations in the French network used for operational avalanche forecasting. Though the weather observations are made there only twice daily, the snow profiles calculated by the model were very close to those obtained once a week by a pit observation. CROCUS proved itself sufficient to be considered now as a useful objective tool for operational avalanche forecasting.",
    "field": "Snowpack modeling",
    "problem": "Existing snow models, designed for hydrological applications, are unsuitable for avalanche forecasting because they do not simulate the evolution of distinct natural snow layers, making the assessment of mechanical stability a difficult and subjective task for forecasters.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a one-dimensional numerical energy and mass balance model, named CROCUS, that simulates the evolution of snow cover layers as a function of meteorological conditions to provide an objective tool for operational avalanche forecasting.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and validate a numerical model, named CROCUS, that simulates the energy and mass evolution of snow cover using meteorological data to support operational avalanche forecasting.",
