    "innovation": "This paper presents the CROCUS model, which simulates the energy and mass evolution of snow cover layers, and validates it as a useful objective tool for operational avalanche forecasting, even when using limited, twice-daily meteorological data.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "APPLIES_TO_REALM",
            "target_entity_instance": "land",
            "target_entity_type": "Realm",
            "paragraph": "ABSTRACT. A numerical model has been developed to simulate energy and mass evolution of snow cover at a given location, as a function of meteorological conditions: precipitation, air temperature, humidity, wind velocity, and incoming short-wave and long-wave radiation.\nThis model, named CROCUS, was first tested on a well-instrumented field during a whole winter, showing its ability to simulate the important phenomena affecting the evolution of the snow layers: high temperature gradients, wetting, compaction, and melting-freezing cycles. A second test was conducted at two locations in the French network used for operational avalanche forecasting. Though the weather observations are made there only twice daily, the snow profiles calculated by the model were very close to those obtained once a week by a pit observation. CROCUS proved itself sufficient to be considered now as a useful objective tool for operational avalanche forecasting.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Stefan-Boltzmann law for long-wave radiation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Long-wave radiation: Ql\nThe radiative properties of snow in the range 5-40 \u03bcm imply radiative exchanges strictly confined to the snow surface.\nQl = \u03b5\u03c2\u03c1\u03b9 \u2013 \u03b5\u03c3\u03c4\nwhere Q is the incident atmospheric long-wave radiation, Ts is snow-surface temperature, o is the Stephan constant, and \u025bs is snow emissivity. Es depends on the wavelength and on the angle of incidence. It generally varies from 0.98 to 1. We chose it equal to 1.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "three-band solar spectrum absorption scheme",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Short-wave radiation: Qs\nIncoming solar radiation is partly reflected by snow. The remaining part penetrates through the snow where it is gradually absorbed. Snow reflectance depends strongly on wavelength, grain-size, and impurity content. As the solar spectrum distribution varies with cloudiness, snow albedo a is not constant for a given snow layer. Absorption \u03b2 depends on wavelength, grain-size, density, and impurities (Bohren and Barkstrom, 1972; Sergent and others, 1987). Since the penetration of solar radiation with depth is an exponential function e-Bz with B varying strongly with wavelength, the solar_absorption cannot be described by a unique mean value B. Therefore, the solar spectrum was divided into three spectral bands, 0.3-0.8, 0.8-1.5, and 1.5- 2.8 \u00b5m, over which a and Bare considered constant and depend only on the grain-size and density according to Sergent (unpublished) and Bohren and Barkstrom (1972).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "modified Deardorff turbulent flux scheme",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Above a snow-covered surface, the air temperature is generally higher than the snow-surface temperature in such a way that the boundary layer is stable. In this case Ch and Ce are very low (Deardoff, 1968) and, under slight wind conditions, the energy supplied to the snow by heat conduction through the air and by vapor diffusion due to vapor gradients in the air may be higher than by turbulent transfer. So, we [the authors of CROCUS] prefer to suggest the following expression for Qh and Qe:\nQh = PaCpaCh(a + bUa) (Ta - Ts)\nQe = (Lspa Mv / Pa Ma) * Ce(a + bUa) (E;(Ta) - E;(Ts))",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Yen (1981) effective snow-conduction formula",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Heat conduction through the snow-pack: Qc\nQc = \u03bb \u2202T/\u2202z\nwhere is the effective snow-conduction coefficient. Yen (1981) showed that most experimental measurements may be described by the following formula:\n\u03bb = \u03bb\u03af (Pn/Pw)^1.88",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Colbeck (1972) water flow formulation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Water movement through the snow-pack\nWhen a snow layer is wet, liquid water may flow downward. According to Colbeck (1972), water flow occurs when water saturation exceeds the irreducible water saturation. The vertical water flux Uw may be expressed as:\nUw = (Pw/\u03bcw) * gk * ((Sw - Swi)/(1-Swi))^3 when Sw > Swi\nUw = 0 when Sw & Swi",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Navarre (1975) snow settling law",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Snow settling\nThe snow layers settle by the combined effect of grain metamorphism and the weight of the upper layers. The latter effect may be described by the mean viscosity. A settling law was established by Navarre (1975): de/e = -\u03c3/\u03b7 * dt ... In the present case, our model [CROCUS] does not describe snow-grain evolution and we therefore considered f(d) constant and equal to 0.4. Such a simplification may have serious consequences regarding the settling of depth-hoar layers, but no satisfactory solution can be found without explicit modeling of snow-grain metamorphism.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "unidimensional",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "This heading describes the choice we have made to take into account all the phenomena affecting the evolution of the snow cover. To simulate a snow-pack, we consider it [the snow-pack] as unidimensional. The energy exchanges are projected perpendicular to the slope and are positive when they are supplied towards the snow-pack.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Cranck and Nicholson implicit method",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To calculate the temperature of each layer, we [the authors of CROCUS] use the classical resolution technique of Cranck and Nicholson which is an implicit method using finite differences. It requires the linearization of each heat exchange with regard to the temperature. The implicit method is necessary to get good accuracy for the exchanges near the surface.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "snow temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "To calculate its [the snow-pack's] temperature, density, and liquid-water content profiles, the snow-pack is divided into layers",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
